{"status":"question","question":{"text":"Good — that gives me a clean spine: CSV in → categorised → \"where my money goes\" view. One load-bearing decision before I name a stack: since it's for you AND your partner, does the data need to live server-side (a shared database you both reach from your own logins/devices), or is a single local instance fine for now? My recommendation: go full-stack with a real database from the start — two people sharing categorised transactions is inherently shared state, and retrofitting a backend later is far more painful than starting with a thin one. That points me at a single TypeScript full-stack app (e.g. Next.js) over a static front-end.","choices":["Shared server-side database (both log in)","Single local instance for now, share later","Not sure — recommend what's simplest to grow"],"recommended":"Shared server-side database (both log in)"},"prd":"# Mission: Personal finance website — foundation\n\n**Type:** feature\n**Goal:** Stand up a running skeleton of a budget & expense tracker with a wired test runner and one thin vertical slice proving they connect.\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- App boots and shows something\n- Test runner wired and watchable by a human\n- One thin slice exercises CSV → categorised → display end-to-end\n**Out of Scope:**\n- Budgets, charts, bank sync (deferred to later missions)\n**Constraints:**\n- TBD (stack pending captain alignment)\n"}

